count(a)==1,a[:60] s=s.repl…", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /hutian/story-wiki && git remote set-url origin $HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/story-repo.wiki.git && git pull -q --rebase 2>&1|tail -1; python3 - <<'E'\np='Outline.md'; s=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"*Rev. 11, by story-repo\",\"*Rev. 12, by story-repo\")\nrep(\"result pending, `story-repo`#7 item d) and the ladder diagnostics placing each matrix; if the OLB training-side prediction misses its empirical k* the paper says the rule is not confirmed.\",\n\"**result in, `story-repo`#7 item d: confirmatory on OLB G (prediction equals the empirical k* at all 7 reachable margins), fails on OLB S (under-predicts by 1–4 grid steps)**) and the ladder diagnostics placing each matrix (asked, `experiment-repo`#8). The paper's claim: the training-side rule is a usable proxy for k*(ε) within the population the model was trained on (MTEB G within one grid step, OLB G exact, one confirmatory test) and is not claimed under shift in strength or time, where it errs in either direction (MTEB T over-predicts by 2–6 steps, OLB S under-predicts by 1–4); the caveat that training and test sides share a population on G stays in the text.\")\nrep(\"### Revision log\\n\",\"### Revision log\\n- rev. 12: OLB training-side k* rule result recorded in C4 (confirmatory: holds on G, fails on S).\\n\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngit add -A && git commit -qm \"Outline rev. 12: OLB training-side rule result in C4\" && git push -q 2>&1|tail -2; git log --oneline|head -1"]], "output": "```console\nfcd0f14 Outline rev. 12: OLB training-side rule result in C4\n\nShell cwd was reset to /hutian/repo\n```", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "502b53bc-1670-54cb-b162-1d9b5c50935f", "at": 1795738064.9847379, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 59611, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "story-repo", "actor": "s